{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1290876-weird-modern-setting-idea-the-horrors-of-the-moon","id":1290876,"title":"Weird Modern Setting Idea: The Horrors of the Moon","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-02-22T12:37:16-08:00","id":1290876,"title":"Weird Modern Setting Idea: The Horrors of the Moon","content":"Basically, I kind of remember that, on the deleted wiki, there was a hack that essentially transported 2E Lunars to the modern day as horror movie monsters. It posited that all myths secretly referred to the same post-human race of shapeshifters, and their creations. Of course, that was building off how Chimerism, may it forever rot, was effectively unavoidable for untattooed Lunars. That didn't sound fun.<br /> <br /> 3E Lunars are effectively even more horror monsters than 2E, given how unpredictable they are and the Monstrous Urge system allowing a lot of personal horror as you wonder how much of that intrusive instinct is born of stress doing unfun things to the balance between human and monster, and how much is your id having freed itself. So, I'm thinking of ways to update that for a more horror focused setting.<br /> <br /> I'm thinking something like the old Nightlife rpg; the Moon Born are born of the internal friction between humanity's higher self and its animal instincts causing some people exposed to the energies of other worlds to lose their shape - but not their minds. The result is something both human and inhuman, monster and hero - angel to some, demon to others. From there, they can reach deep into the wild depths of the unconscious to unleash wonders and terrors on the world (I imagine being in a Beast Soul Awakening Crucible here is even more like stepping into R'lyeh). It's both gift and curse. On the one hand, being a shapeshifter with the potential to be a god is pretty awesome. On the other hand, you aren't human any more; the Monstrous Urge drives you to keep moving, keep changing, lest you succumb to the inner chaos and wake up day later with a bloody knife, no memories, and wanted by the cops, and you have the <i>potential</i> of godhood. Others, like the enigmatic and aloof sahan-yahs, have already reached it, and those ancient king-beasts are as distant and strange as any god. To say nothing of monsters who were never human, and don't especially care to learn humanity...<br /> <br /> Edit: Quick note: the sahan-yahs here are technically a stand-in for vampire elders, but I'm thinking more along the lines of 2E Requiem elders or Nightlife's own Goltholga; the &quot;ancient unstoppable horror&quot; is about 60% PR, they're simply politically powerful people who know their positions were won by merit and can be taken by merit. The 40% is god-monster, because these people think the Thing was woefully limited by special effects and imagination, but they aren't invincible author-inserts. The players are expected to view them as mentors and ignorable important characters, not ways for the Storyteller to beat the world into the shape she wants.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":180,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","user":"Leliel","date":". 02-22-2019, 03:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}